  "prompt": "Draw a detailed educational diagram for: Average Velocity and Instantaneous Velocity. Context: This topic covers two fundamental concepts in kinematics that describe how velocity is measured over different time scales. Average velocity is defined for a time interval and represents the overall rate of change of position. It is calculated as the displacement divided by the time interval, giving the net effect of motion without considering variations during the interval. Only the initial and final positions matter for average velocity calculation - the actual path taken is irrelevant. Instantaneous velocity, on the other hand, is defined at a specific instant in time and represents the rat",
